From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:50:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] A sprite is not geometry

Patch 0015 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

Patch 0014's rungs did not remove what the user calls the navi shadow conflict: on rung 20, with
the newest build, a dark RECTANGLE still sits behind the fairy with her glow inside it and the
hedge either side of it lit normally. A rectangle that follows nothing in the world is a quad.

Every rung since patch 0012 decides a draw is geometry rather than a sprite by asking for more
than TWO triangles, and two is a very low bar. A fairy's glow is a handful of layered quads, not
two, so it has been passing that test and being reclassified as solid all along. A sprite treated
as solid is rasterized before the composite and therefore multiplied by the surface's shadow
factor, which is the exact fault that putting translucent draws after the composite was written to
fix, and the exact thing being reported.

These two rungs raise the bar, bracketed rather than guessed at one value, because the right cut
sits somewhere between a sprite and a tree: a pine is a few crossed quads and a hedge line is
many. If the fairy comes right at one of them and the trees stay lit, the bar is the lever and the
value is whichever of the two holds both.

It is offered as the likeliest reading of one frame rather than as a diagnosis. If neither rung
holds both, the instrument rather than another reading: the Hits view at that spot says whether
the rectangle is a traced hit at all.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1886,6 +1886,20 @@ namespace RT64 {
                             // out by a partial coverage. 20 keeps the shadow off, 21 puts it back.
                             case 20: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; castsNothing = true; break;
                             case 21: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
+                            // 22 and 23 RAISE THE BAR that decides a draw is geometry rather than
+                            // a sprite (patch 0015). Every rung above says "more than two
+                            // triangles", and two is a very low bar: a fairy's glow is a handful
+                            // of layered quads, not two, so she has been passing it. A sprite
+                            // reclassified as solid is rasterized BEFORE the composite and so is
+                            // multiplied by the surface's shadow factor, which is exactly the
+                            // fault the translucent-after-composite work fixed in the first place,
+                            // and exactly what "the navi shadow conflict" describes.
+                            //
+                            // Bracketed rather than guessed at one number, because the right cut
+                            // sits somewhere between a sprite and a tree and neither of us knows
+                            // where: a pine is a few crossed quads, a hedge line is many.
+                            case 22: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && (call.callDesc.triangleCount > 4); castsNothing = true; break;
+                            case 23: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && (call.callDesc.triangleCount > 16); castsNothing = true; break;
                             default: solidDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER); break;
                         }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
